{
  // Detection-benchmark ground truth — ADJUDICATED, not auto-calibrated.
  //
  // Rebuilt 2026-07-25 at commit c3069ec (binary sha256 391fd747d15ad17c, built in an
  // isolated worktree so no other agent's rebuild could move it mid-run). The previous file predated the
  // v0.23.0 rule renaming and could not be scored at all.
  //
  // KEY FORMAT: "<sourceId>/<tree-relative path>:<line>" -> [ruleId, ...]. The sourceId prefix is
  // load-bearing (see scripts/measure/benchmark.mjs): tree-relative keys collapse locations in different
  // trees onto one key, silently turning a miss in one tree into a hit from another.
  //
  // 2026-07-26 KEY CHANGE -- THE "<cross>/..." BUCKET IS GONE. It used to hold all 17 relational
  // cross-layer findings, because benchmark.mjs derived a finding's tree from `data.source` and only the
  // single-tree cross-layer rules emit that field. benchmark.mjs now walks a documented fallback chain
  // (`data.source` -> `consumeSource` -> `patternSource` -> the site entry matching the anchor's
  // file:line -> `sources[0]`; see `attributeCross` there for why each step names the ANCHOR's tree and
  // not the other side of the join). 15 of the 17 now carry a real sourceId prefix and were re-keyed
  // below; 2 remained unattributable and moved to an explicit "<unattributed>/" bucket that benchmark.mjs
  // PRINTS with its reason on every run, so a score can never read as "everything was attributed":
  //   * cross-layer/external-shadow-internal -- data named only `matchedProvide.source`, the PROVIDE
  //     side; the anchor is the CONSUME site, whose source the payload never emitted.
  //   * cross-layer/external-host-fanout -- `exampleFiles` were bare paths with no source at all.
  // Both were rule-side gaps (each rule HAD the value in hand: `c.source` in external_shadow_internal.rs,
  // `site.source` in external_host_fanout.rs) -- routed to rules-owner.
  // CLOSED in the v0.24.0 batch: both rules now emit the anchor's source, the fallback chain resolves
  // both, and the "<unattributed>/" bucket is EMPTY (see the re-keying note above the first entry). The
  // bucket and benchmark.mjs's printing of it stay, so a future attribution gap still announces itself.
  //
  // The re-keying is itself evidence the collision risk was live rather than theoretical: FOUR of the
  // 15 landed on a key that ALREADY existed and had to be merged into it --
  // xfe/consumes.ts:7, xfe/consumes.ts:8, xbe2/provides.ts:13, api/src/queries.ts:22.
  //
  // HOW THIS WAS ADJUDICATED (the point of the file -- "it fires, so it is correct" is forbidden):
  //   (The 69/74 split below is the measurement at the 2026-07-25 rebuild -- a historical record of that
  //   adjudication. The label set has grown since, so these counts differing from today's totals is
  //   normal, not drift.)
  //   * 69 findings landed in per-rule modules whose FILENAME declares the rule under test
  //     (trees/<repo>/{rules,api,services}/<pack>.<rule>.ts). Each was confirmed by reading the anchored
  //     source line and checking it sits in the module's `bad` export, not its `good` control. The
  //     filename is independent evidence: it states the planted defect and predates the run.
  //   * 74 findings (cross-layer, graph, callgraph, schema, and cross-module co-fires) were confirmed
  //     against the anchored source line directly; the corpus's own inline comments state the intended
  //     verdict at most of those anchors and were checked to agree.
  //   * A `good` export is a negative control for THAT MODULE'S rule only. A few findings land in a
  //     `good` region under a DIFFERENT rule and are kept as true positives, because each verdict is
  //     independently correct there: sql/select-star at raw-query-interpolation.ts:14 (the safe
  //     `$queryRaw` tagged template still selects `*`), and cross-layer/unconsumed-endpoint on the
  //     good routes of protected-path-no-auth-evidence/dev-path-no-guard-hint
  //     (those routes really have no consumer).
  //
  // RESOLVED 2026-07-26 -- THE ONE WITHHELD FIRING IS GONE. `http/protected-path-no-auth-evidence` used to
  // fire inside `export function good()` of api/api/http.protected-path-no-auth-evidence.ts, and was
  // deliberately kept OUT of this file so it
  // scored as a false positive instead of becoming truth. The root cause was a CORPUS gap, not a rule bug:
  // that `good` tried to clear the rule by naming its handler `requireAdminListUsers`, while the matcher
  // (rules/dsl/http/http.json) is an io-scan over `provides` keyed on `/(admin|internal)/` with
  // `attr_absent: "auth-guarded"` that never reads a handler identifier. The only clearing mechanism is an
  // injected `auth-guarded` attribute, and nothing in the corpus injected one. The module now carries two
  // real negative controls fed by the Mode B adapter overlay this corpus already wires up
  // (zzop-attributes.json, declared BOTH in trees/api-be/zzop.config.jsonc for the analyze_repo axis and in
  // the outer zzop.config.jsonc for the join): an exact `ioKey` target on `GET /admin/reports` and a
  // `pathScope` target on prefix `/admin/audit`. Both go silent; the bad route still fires. This rule's
  // precision is now measured rather than assumed.
  //
  // STILL OPEN, FOR rules-owner (recorded here because the corpus is where it surfaced):
  // `protected-path-no-auth-evidence` and `dev-path-no-guard-hint` are siblings in one pack, matched with
  // the same io-scan shape over the same registration lines, but they accept DIFFERENT clear-mechanisms --
  // dev-path-no-guard-hint reads the source LINE (`anchor_exclude_pattern` guard-hint keywords, plus a
  // suppress marker), protected-path-no-auth-evidence reads ONLY injected
  // attributes. A corpus author reasonably expects the same escape hatch from both, which is precisely the
  // mistake the old `good` encoded. Intended, or an inconsistency worth closing?
  //
  // NOT RESTORED -- 2 expectations from the old file are gone by design, not by regression:
  //   cross-layer/unconsumed-endpoint at callgraph/writeEndpoint.ts:11 and xbe/provides.ts:8. Both are
  //   write routes, and cross-layer/unconsumed-mutation-endpoint (which still fires at both anchors) is a
  //   strict specialization that REPLACES the generic rule on the routes it reports -- stated in
  //   rules/native/rules-cross-layer/src/cross_layer/unconsumed_endpoint.rs:10-15 and
  //   .../unconsumed_mutation_endpoint.rs:9. Re-adding them would manufacture two permanent false negatives.
  //
  // REGENERATING: do NOT run `--write-expected` against a fresh binary and commit the result. That
  // converts the benchmark into a machine that re-confirms current output, and its regression-detection
  // value goes to zero. Re-adjudicate changed anchors by hand.

  // RE-KEYED 2026-07-26 (v0.24.0 batch): the "<unattributed>/" bucket is now EMPTY. Both rule-side gaps
  // named in the header note were closed in that batch -- `external_shadow_internal.rs` now emits the
  // CONSUME side's `source`, and `external_host_fanout.rs` now carries `(source, file)` per site -- so
  // benchmark.mjs's fallback chain resolves both anchors to a real tree. Re-measured, not regenerated:
  // the anchors (file:line) are byte-identical to round 7, only the tree attribution improved, and the
  // external-host-fanout entry MERGED into the api key that already existed at the same anchor.
  "api/api/be-reliability.fetch-no-timeout.ts:9": ["cross-layer/external-host-fanout","reliability/fetch-no-timeout"],
  // Re-keyed 2026-07-26: the module grew two attribute-cleared negative controls, so every anchor shifted
