  // MERGED KEY (was "<cross>/src/queries.ts:22"): the db-table-name-in-multiple-sources anchor is this exact line.
  // `table:order` is consumed by both `api` and `worker`, and since 2026-07-29 the rule emits ONE COPY PER
  // PARTICIPATING SOURCE, each anchored in that source's own tree — so this key and `worker/jobs.ts:8` below are a
  // pair, not a duplicate. It used to anchor only at the alphabetically-first source, which meant `api` excluding its
  // own paths deleted the finding about `worker` too.
  "api/src/queries.ts:22": ["cross-layer/db-table-name-in-multiple-sources","orderby-unindexed"],
  // ---- THE AMBIGUITY TRIPLE (added 2026-07-29 with cross-layer/all-consumes-unjoined) -------------
  // `be-catalog-a` and `be-catalog-b` implement one IDENTICAL three-route API and `fe-catalog` calls it.
  // Adjudicated by reading each anchored line, not by trusting the run:
  //   * duplicate-route x3 PER SOURCE -- both provides.ts files register the same three keys, verified
  //     line by line, and since 2026-07-29 the rule emits ONE COPY PER PROVIDING SOURCE, each anchored in
  //     that source's own tree (the same change db-table-name-in-multiple-sources took above). So `cata`
  //     and `catb` each carry three, and excluding one backend's paths no longer deletes the finding
  //     about the other.
  //   * mutating-route-no-auth on each `POST /catalog/orders` -- neither handler is guarded, and neither
  //     path matches the auth-acquisition vocabulary. Same verdict xbe/provides.ts:8 already carries.
  //   * all-consumes-unjoined at the FIRST of fe-catalog's three calls -- all three keys resolve cleanly
  //     and all three match two provider trees, so the linker emits no edge for any of them. This is the
  //     shape four of the six real dogfood front ends have, and the reason the rule exists.
  // What is NOT here is the point of the fixture: `cross-layer/ambiguous-consume` would have fired once
  // per call site and is REPLACED by the single fold. Disabling all-consumes-unjoined hands those three
  // back, so a regression that breaks the replacement contract shows up as three FPs here.
  "cata/provides.ts:13": ["cross-layer/duplicate-route"],
  "cata/provides.ts:14": ["cross-layer/duplicate-route"],
  "cata/provides.ts:15": ["cross-layer/duplicate-route","mutating-route-no-auth"],
  "catb/provides.ts:7": ["cross-layer/duplicate-route"],
  "catb/provides.ts:8": ["cross-layer/duplicate-route"],
  "catb/provides.ts:9": ["cross-layer/duplicate-route","mutating-route-no-auth"],
  "fecat/consumes.ts:16": ["cross-layer/all-consumes-unjoined"],
  // ---- end ambiguity triple ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  "callgraph/readEndpoint.ts:12": ["cross-layer/unconsumed-endpoint"],
  "callgraph/readEndpoint.ts:8": ["unsafe-read-endpoint"],
  "callgraph/writeEndpoint.ts:11": ["cross-layer/unconsumed-mutation-endpoint","mutating-route-no-auth"],
  "callgraph/writeEndpoint.ts:8": ["non-idempotent-write"],

  // ---- THE TWO NEGATIVE-CASE PAIRS (added 2026-07-29) --------------------------------------------
  // Added because this corpus scored TP 143 FN 0 FP 0 -- 100% recall AND 100% precision -- while only 2
  // of 6 front-end trees in the real dogfood corpus joined at all. The cause was that EVERY front-end
  // consume here was a LITERAL PATH: the answer key was planted exclusively INSIDE the capability
  // boundary, so 100% measured the inside of the boundary rather than the capability. Nothing in
  // trees/ contained a cross-file `baseURL` assignment.
  //
  // Two pairs now sit deliberately OUTSIDE it. Each is a front end whose calls carry only the path
  // SUFFIX, paired with a backend that genuinely provides the full route -- so the correct answer is a
  // join and the current answer is a miss:
  //   * feaxios x bearticles  -- `axios.defaults.baseURL = settings.baseApiUrl`, a cross-file constant.
  //     parser/.../adapters/client_base.rs recognizes a string LITERAL only and names this exact
  //     expression as the shape it refuses (never-guess IO convention).
  //   * fegensdk x beinvoices -- a vendored swagger `HttpClient` whose `baseUrl` is read from the
  //     environment. parser/.../adapters/client_base_generated.rs refuses that the same way.
  //
  // BOTH ARE NOW REPAIRED IN THE CONFIG, not in the recognizers (2026-07-29). Neither refusal was ever a
  // bug -- guessing a base mis-keys every consume in the tree -- so the fix was to let the author STATE
  // the base: `trees[].topology.clientBase`, declared on each front end in zzop.config.jsonc. What this
  // fixture measures therefore changed on purpose, from "how big is the hole" to "does the declared
  // repair actually work end to end". The hole itself has not moved: delete either declaration and every
  // expectation below reverts.
  //
  // The declaration also carries the idempotence pin. Each front end ALSO calls the same four routes with
  // full literal paths from server/loader.ts (the Next-style split: a browser client with an implicit
  // base, server-side rendering with `fetch` and no client instance). Those 8 calls join today and must
  // keep joining -- a blind prepend would re-key them to /api/api/... and this gate would report 8 lost
  // joins. See crates/engine/src/analyze/compose/config_client_base.rs, which diverges from the
  // code-extracted pass for exactly this reason.
  //
  // HOW AN UNMET EXPECTATION IS SPELLED HERE. Until 2026-07-29 this harness had exactly two
  // dispositions -- an expectation (absent -> FN) and a `benign` control (present -> FP) -- and no third
  // state for "correct, not yet implemented" (`untracked` annotates a FN's cause but still counts it and
  // still exits nonzero). So these two were written as ORDINARY expectations and the gate was
  // PERMANENTLY RED: red for something nobody intended to fix that week, which blocks every push and
  // teaches everyone to ignore the gate. Writing them as `benign` instead was never an option -- "no
  // finding here is correct" would freeze today's incapacity as the right answer forever, which is the
  // exact reason these cases were added.
  //
  // They lived in the `gap` bucket at the bottom of this file, the third disposition
  // scripts/measure/benchmark.mjs grew that day: excluded from TP/FN/FP, printed as `GAP n/N closed` on
  // its own line in every run, exit-ZERO while open, and exit-NONZERO the day one fires so that progress
  // is promoted by hand rather than absorbed. The coverage floor carries a third column so a gap cannot
  // be deleted silently -- a gap costs nothing in the score, so without that column its removal would be
  // completely invisible.
  //
  // THE TWO `cross-layer/method-mismatch` ENTRIES BELOW ARE THAT PROMOTION, and the mechanism worked
  // exactly as designed: the run that shipped `clientBase` printed `GAP 2/2 closed` and ABORTED, refusing
  // to score until a human moved these two lines out of `gap`. Both backends provide their fourth route
  // as GET only while the front end POSTs to it, so with the base resolved each is a real method mismatch
  // anchored at the consume. Both anchors ALREADY carried `unprovided-mutation-call` (which co-fires with
  // method-mismatch by that rule's own module doc), so the promotion was purely additive.
  //
  // THE `cross-layer/prefix-drift` ENTRIES ARE GONE, and their deletion is the other half of the same
  // hand edit. They were the DEGRADE path and true positives while the base was unresolved: three
  // same-method reads per pair landed as `prefix`-dimension route-near-misses that aggregated into one
  // finding naming the missing `/api`. The declaration resolves the base, the three reads JOIN, and there
  // is no drift left to name. `--update-baseline` grows only, so retiring an expectation is deliberately
  // a hand edit of scripts/detection-expected-baseline.txt -- the ratchet does not let a detection be
  // given up without an author. They got no inverse of `gap` ("will stop firing"), and that was
  // considered and declined the same day: the scorer cannot tell "it vanished because the base resolved"
  // from "it vanished because the near-miss aggregation regressed", so such a marker would convert the
  // ratchet's fail-closed edge into a fail-open one on the recall axis it exists to protect. Adjudicating
  // by hand, once, in the commit that caused it is the cost of that safety -- and this is that commit.
  "feaxios/pages/articles.ts:43": ["cross-layer/unprovided-mutation-call","cross-layer/method-mismatch"],
  "fegensdk/api/Api.ts:21": ["cross-layer/unprovided-mutation-call","cross-layer/method-mismatch"],
  // ---- end negative-case pairs -------------------------------------------------------------------

  "graph/circularA.ts:1": ["circular"],
  "graph/duplicateRoute.ts:6": ["cross-layer/unconsumed-endpoint"],
  "graph/duplicateRoute.ts:7": ["cross-layer/unconsumed-endpoint","duplicate-route"],
  "graph/routeShadowing.ts:10": ["cross-layer/unconsumed-endpoint"],
  "graph/routeShadowing.ts:11": ["cross-layer/unconsumed-endpoint","route-shadowing"],
  "island/deadA.ts:1": ["circular","unreachable"],
  "island/deadB.ts:1": ["unreachable"],
  "island/orphan.ts:1": ["unreachable"],
  // ---- THE JAVA LANE OF FOUR MULTI-LANGUAGE RULES (added 2026-07-29) -----------------------------
  // Fifteen `security` rules admit `.java`. Eleven had a Java fixture (UnsafeController/UserRepository
  // below); these four admitted `.java` in their file_pattern but were only ever exercised on TypeScript,
  // so the Java half of each regex shipped unmeasured. Adjudicated by reading each anchored line — every
  // one sits on a planted defect whose `good` counterpart is the next line and correctly does NOT fire.
  //   * :14 a secret-named field assigned a literal (the next line reads System.getenv instead)
  //   * :19 user:password inside a postgresql:// URI
  //   * :24 an api_key travelling as a query parameter
  //   * :29 MD5 over a password — weak-crypto CO-FIRES here and that is correct, not a duplicate: MD5 is
  //     both a weak digest and the wrong choice for passwords, and the two rules say different things.
  // The Java console lane of the call-site channel (W2) — this tree, not api-be, because its files
  // already sit under a src/main/java layout and the fixture only needs a services/ path segment for
  // console-in-be's backend-path gate; the env-read lane cannot live here (no env-config-module
  // attribute is declared for this tree, so env-outside-config does not run — see the api-be twin).
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/services/ConsoleHygiene.java:9": ["reliability/console-in-be"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/services/ConsoleHygiene.java:14": ["reliability/console-in-be","reliability/console-in-loop"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/CredentialsConfig.java:14": ["security/hardcoded-secret"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/CredentialsConfig.java:19": ["security/conn-string-credentials"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/CredentialsConfig.java:24": ["security/api-key-in-url"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/CredentialsConfig.java:29": ["security/weak-crypto","security/weak-password-hash"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/UnsafeController.java:19": ["security/sql-string-concat"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/UnsafeController.java:25": ["security/weak-crypto"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/UnsafeController.java:30": ["security/cmd-injection"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/UnsafeController.java:35": ["security/hardcoded-password"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/UnsafeController.java:40": ["security/xxe-no-guard"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/UnsafeController.java:46": ["security/unsafe-deserialization"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/UnsafeController.java:51": ["security/java-path-traversal"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/UnsafeController.java:56": ["security/weak-random"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/UnsafeController.java:62": ["security/stacktrace-to-response"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/UnsafeController.java:68": ["security/trust-all-tls"],
  "java/src/main/java/com/example/svc/UserRepository.java:8": ["security/annotation-sql-concat"],
  // ---- THE RUST LANE (added 2026-08-02) -----------------------------------------------------------
  // Before this tree existed, ZERO of the 138 shipped DSL rules admitted a `.rs` file (java 18, ts 117),
  // so a Rust-only tree got the D16 "no applicable DSL rules" self-report and nothing else. Fifteen rules
  // were widened and three Rust-native ones added; the fifteen were chosen one at a time by reading the
  // MATCHER, not by extension-globbing — the standing rule being that a pattern written in JS/TS
  // vocabulary (`process.env`, `console.`, `Math.random`, `===`) that merely admits `.rs` is a rule that
  // is FOREVER SILENT there while the catalog claims it runs. Each anchor below was
  // confirmed by reading the anchored source line and checking it sits in the module's planted-defect
  // half, with the `good_*` counterpart directly beside it silent — the same adjudication the java lane
  // above got, and the reason the fixture files pair every bad shape with its nearest benign lookalike.
  //
  // The two anchors that are NOT a plain line scan are load-bearing and were placed deliberately:
  // `security/command-and-interpolation` and `reliability/reqwest-no-timeout` are method-scans, so they
  // need the Rust parser's `SourceSymbol` body spans. If `syn` ever stops parsing `handlers.rs`, those two
  // go silent while every lexical anchor in this tree keeps scoring — which is precisely why one file in
  // the tree had to be span-dependent rather than line-dependent.
  //
  // What is deliberately NOT here: `security/vendor-token-committed` also had `.rs` added (its matcher is
  // a contiguous-literal scan with no language token in it at all), and it has no fixture in this tree for
  // the SAME reason its TypeScript fixture cannot be committed — see the `untracked` note above. Its Rust
  // lane is therefore extension-widened but corpus-unmeasured, and this paragraph is that disclosure.
  "rust/src/config.rs:6": ["egress/http-url-literal"],
  "rust/src/config.rs:11": ["egress/localhost-url-literal-committed"],
  "rust/src/config.rs:16": ["security/cors-wildcard"],
  "rust/src/config.rs:30": ["reliability/debug-true-committed"],
  // 2026-08-02 (U62): csp-weak-or-disabled widened to `.rs` — its unsafe-inline / default-src-wildcard
  // arms are wire-header VALUE shapes. Each bad line has a strict-policy good twin right below it.
  "rust/src/config.rs:44": ["security/csp-weak-or-disabled"],
  "rust/src/config.rs:52": ["security/csp-weak-or-disabled"],
  // 2026-08-03: cors-wildcard grew Rust idiom ARMS (matched by their own spelling, not the wire
  // header): tower-http `.allow_origin(Any)`, actix `.allow_any_origin()`, and the typed constant
  // `ACCESS_CONTROL_ALLOW_ORIGIN` beside a `"*"` literal. Each bad line has its nearest-benign good
  // twin directly below it (`AllowOrigin::exact`, `allowed_origin`+`allow_any_method/header`, the
  // same constant beside an allow-listed value), so the arms are pinned against the lookalikes most
  // likely to false-positive.
  "rust/src/config.rs:65": ["security/cors-wildcard"],
  "rust/src/config.rs:73": ["security/cors-wildcard"],
  "rust/src/config.rs:81": ["security/cors-wildcard"],
  "rust/src/credentials.rs:11": ["security/hardcoded-secret"],
  // The SECOND arm of the same rule, not a duplicate: `assignment` needs the value to follow the name
  // directly, and Rust's `const API_KEY: &str = "…"` puts a type in between, so it took a `rust-str-const`
  // arm of its own. Deleting either arm loses exactly one of these two lines.
  "rust/src/credentials.rs:22": ["security/hardcoded-secret"],
  "rust/src/credentials.rs:25": ["security/conn-string-credentials"],
  "rust/src/credentials.rs:29": ["security/api-key-in-url"],
  "rust/src/credentials.rs:34": ["security/private-key-committed"],
  "rust/src/credentials.rs:40": ["security/weak-password-hash"],
  // 2026-08-03: sql-format-interpolation's matcher now requires the template to BEGIN with the
  // statement and accepts the `{}` placeholder on EITHER side of the statement keywords. These two are
  // the directions the original after-the-keyword matcher could not see (dynamic column list, dynamic
  // UPDATE table); the same file's unlabeled `cache_delete_error` is the log-line lookalike that fired
  // before the begins-with anchor and must stay silent now, and rust/migrations/v2_backfill.rs (in
  // `benign` below) pins the `${test-paths-migrations}` exclusion this rule now shares with its
  // `sql/` no-where siblings.
  "rust/src/dynamic_queries.rs:10": ["security/sql-format-interpolation"],
  "rust/src/dynamic_queries.rs:20": ["security/sql-format-interpolation"],
  "rust/src/handlers.rs:11": ["security/command-and-interpolation"],
  "rust/src/handlers.rs:25": ["reliability/reqwest-no-timeout"],
  // 2026-08-03: reqwest-no-timeout's trigger grew `reqwest::get` (a fresh timeout-less default client
  // per call) and its veto tightened from the bare word `timeout` to the method spelling `.timeout(` —
  // so a connect_timeout-only builder, which caps the HANDSHAKE and not the stalled-peer request, now
  // fires. The good twin beside them carries a request-level `.timeout(` on an otherwise-flaggable
  // client and must stay silent.
  "rust/src/handlers.rs:43": ["reliability/reqwest-no-timeout"],
  "rust/src/handlers.rs:47": ["reliability/reqwest-no-timeout"],
  // `inline_fixtures.rs` scores the test-region gate in BOTH directions, out of one `#[cfg(test)] mod
  // tests`. THREE of its four literals must stay unscored — `sql/select-star`, `sql/delete-no-where`,
  // `egress/localhost-url-literal-committed` judge code that RUNS, and the gate
  // (`zzop_parser_rust::extract_test_spans` -> `SourceFile::test_spans` -> `dsl::eval`) is what keeps
  // them out; a gate that stops working turns them into three FALSE POSITIVES here rather than into
  // silence somewhere nobody reads. Seen red on purpose — removing BOTH test attributes takes this run
  // from FP 0 to FP 3 (removing only the `cfg` one does NOT, which is a trap that file's own header
  // records). The FOURTH is scored below: `security/hardcoded-secret` declares `scan_test_regions`
  // (2026-08-02), because a credential at rest is leaked by the commit and no test attribute un-commits
  // it — so its silence would be an FN, not a pass. That line was deliberately unlabeled before the flag
  // existed, which is precisely the wrong half of the behavior this corpus was pinning. `:43` is the
  // SHIPPED control in the same file: without it, a rule that simply died on `.rs` would look identical
  // to a working gate.
  "rust/src/inline_fixtures.rs:43": ["sql/select-star"],
  "rust/src/inline_fixtures.rs:53": ["security/hardcoded-secret"],
  "rust/src/queries.rs:13": ["sql/select-star"],
  "rust/src/queries.rs:23": ["sql/delete-no-where"],
  "rust/src/queries.rs:32": ["sql/update-no-where"],
  "rust/src/queries.rs:41": ["sql/truncate-in-app-code"],
  "rust/src/queries.rs:47": ["sql/like-leading-wildcard"],
  "rust/src/queries.rs:57": ["security/sql-format-interpolation"],
  // The partition pin (see the fixture's own header): an UPDATE template whose `{}` sits between
  // inner `'` quotes. ONE label on purpose — `sql/update-no-where` co-firing here (it once did: the
  // inner `'` read as the literal's closing quote hid the placeholder from its exclusion) is the
  // placeholder partition breaking, and it surfaces in this gate as an FP, not as a relabel.
  "rust/src/queries.rs:71": ["security/sql-format-interpolation"],
  // ---- end rust lane -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "sdk/billingPage.ts:1": ["cross-layer/untraced-client-import-no-visible-consume"],
  "trpc/router.ts:9": ["cross-layer/unconsumed-procedure"],
  "unresolved/gateway.ts:8": ["cross-layer/unresolved-consume-ratio"],
  "unresolved/gateway.ts:9": ["cross-layer/method-mismatch"],
